// spec ch16 §16.2(2): an integer-literal may take any type in Table 1
// other than `bool` and `char`, and §16.2's note names this exact
// spelling -- "`char c = 65;` ... ill-formed". Characterisation: the
// rejection predates this change; what moved was the sentence, which
// used to name a source type ("a 'int' value") the literal never had.
int main() {
    char c = 65;
    return c == 65 ? 0 : 1;
}
